WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.599 --> 00:00:06.680
There is a stunning new poll out of Texas,
which is also sort of not stunning if you've

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:09.110
been paying attention for the last 10 years.

00:00:09.110 --> 00:00:14.280
And what the poll tells us is that Texas is
no longer a safe Trump state in 2020.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:17.970
This is extraordinary and the implications
are wide reaching.

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:25.690
There's a new Univision poll which shows that
40% of registered Texas voters now say they

00:00:25.690 --> 00:00:30.520
will vote for the Democrat that ultimately
wins the primary instead of Donald Trump.

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:35.109
Only 33% say that they will vote for Donald
Trump in Texas.

00:00:35.109 --> 00:00:40.769
Once you include undecideds who are leaning
one way or the other, the advantage definitely

00:00:40.769 --> 00:00:41.769
shrinks.

00:00:41.769 --> 00:00:45.790
It becomes Democrat 47 Trump 42.

00:00:45.790 --> 00:00:48.120
Now that is only 89% total.

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:50.389
That's still leaves 11% gray area.

00:00:50.389 --> 00:00:56.049
But it doesn't matter because these numbers
are already even accounting for that 11% that

00:00:56.049 --> 00:00:58.089
is fully undecided.

00:00:58.089 --> 00:01:01.570
This is already a massive change from years
past.

00:01:01.570 --> 00:01:04.780
This is something we've been talking about
for a very long time.

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:11.390
Texas could become a swing state, a purple
state, and Donald Trump might be accelerating

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:13.560
that to happen even sooner.

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:18.880
And a big part of that, as we've been following
for years, will be the effect of Hispanic

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:26.290
voters who plan to vote for the Democrat,
whoever it is, over Donald Trump by a 69 to

00:01:26.290 --> 00:01:34.190
19 margin in Texas, that is a 50 point margin
to Donald Trump's disadvantage among Hispanic

00:01:34.190 --> 00:01:35.570
voters in Texas.

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:38.109
Now, let's be crystal clear.

00:01:38.109 --> 00:01:44.369
This does not mean I expect Donald Trump to
lose Texas in 2020 Republicans, uh, when Texas

00:01:44.369 --> 00:01:50.579
often they've been winning it for a very long
time, but if it's even remotely this close,

00:01:50.579 --> 00:01:53.649
it signals really bad things for Donald Trump.

00:01:53.649 --> 00:01:54.649
Nationally.

00:01:54.649 --> 00:01:59.100
In 2016, Donald Trump won Texas over Hillary
by nine points, okay?

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:05.960
52 to 43 in 2012 even though Barack Obama
won hugely at the national level being reelected,

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:13.970
of course Barack Obama Lost Texas to Mitt
Romney 57 to 41 a 16 point deficit.

00:02:13.970 --> 00:02:20.040
What that tells us is that if it's even close
in Texas, if Trump wins Texas, but only by

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:27.000
two three four five it is really bad in terms
of what it signals for Donald Trump nationally,

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:30.630
and this is actually Texas is sandwiched in
a bigger story which is developing, which

00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:36.040
is that if we get the vote out and that is
a very big if there, there's a massive opportunity

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:39.630
to put in play states that are not usually
in play.

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:45.830
All those possibly future purple states are
potentially winnable in 2020 Georgia could

00:02:45.830 --> 00:02:47.100
be in play.

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:52.760
Trump won Georgia in 2016 but he trails a
generic Democratic challenger there.

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:56.830
According to a poll from eight weeks ago,
North Carolina, same thing.

00:02:56.830 --> 00:03:03.680
Donald Trump won it in 2016 Trump is losing
by five points to win a as yet unnamed generic

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:05.640
future Democratic nominee.

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:10.490
Then you've got Pennsylvania, Wisconsin, and
Michigan, which are always in play for the

00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:14.410
Democratic candidate if we actually go and
try to win them.

00:03:14.410 --> 00:03:20.090
The polling even in Florida is not super recent,
but Biden was plus three over Trump.

00:03:20.090 --> 00:03:24.850
Uh, Bernie was up a similar margin over Trump
the last time that poll was done.

00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:26.970
This is not a foregone conclusion.

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:30.170
Trump could also win reelection, but this
is just a matter of do we get the vote out?

00:03:30.170 --> 00:03:35.400
So when you hear me talk about this, please
don't react by thinking, oh, we've got everything.

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:36.400
We don't need to work.

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:37.400
We don't need to vote.

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:38.400
We don't need to go and get the vote out.

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:42.680
The reaction should be, wow, Donald Trump
is unpopular in many red states.

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:48.220
If we work incredibly hard, if we mobilize
people who already don't like Trump and agree

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:50.800
with us on policy, but often don't vote.

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:56.200
If we mobilize them to go out and vote, then
we have a shot at some of these states.

00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:58.450
And that should be the attitude.

00:03:58.450 --> 00:04:02.500
And this polling only supports that view if
we don't get the vote out.

00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:03.500
None of this stuff matters.

00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:07.570
It becomes completely academic and it becomes
another four years of Donald Trump saying,

00:04:07.570 --> 00:04:08.670
the polls said this

00:04:08.670 --> 00:04:12.620
before the election and I want again, I don't
want that to happen.

00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:15.290
I know most people in the audience don't want
that to happen.

00:04:15.290 --> 00:04:16.359
The opportunity is there.

00:04:16.359 --> 00:04:16.729
We've got to get the vote out.

